"Expresses a hypothesis about how variables influence one another through reinforcing and balancing feedback loops.\n\nUse when: Discuss why a recurring pattern persists despite local interventions. Expose delayed and unintended consequences of policy changes.\n\nAvoid when: The team needs numerical predictions without a quantified, validated model.\n\nVisual grammar: Variables have names that can meaningfully increase or decrease. Signed arrows indicate same-direction or opposite-direction effects, all else equal. Loop markers distinguish reinforcing and balancing feedback; delays are explicit.\n\nBuild: Define the dynamic question and select variables with clear meanings. Draw defensible causal links and assign their polarity. Trace each loop and split dense feedback structures into focused views.\n\nCheck: Polarity refers to a causal change, not whether an effect is desirable. Loop classification matches the number of negative links. Preserve real feedback through separate views instead of removing crossings.\n\nPitfalls: Treating correlation as sufficient evidence of causation. Claiming a qualitative loop diagram predicts timing or magnitude.\n\nExample: A volunteer program connects workload, fatigue, retention and remaining workload, revealing a reinforcing cycle and a delayed training response.\n\nAlternatives: Stock-and-flow diagram, Interrelationship diagram."
